    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 15,
        "sourceEnd": 492,
        "text": "Good morning, everyone. Today I want to talk about a question that comes up in every field: is the newest idea always the best one? Let me start with a small story. Last year, a student group at our university built a very fancy app for finding study partners. It had bright colors, funny sounds, and a robot helper. Everyone loved it for two weeks. Then people stopped using it. Why? Because it did not help students actually meet. The app was novel, but it was not effective.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "06e2ed6f5ceb48ceae016241842d6d2ce84dfd049c96a197a6fa0228a6daba0a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 494,
        "sourceEnd": 575,
        "text": "Student A (Mia): Professor, sorry to interrupt. So you are saying novelty is bad?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AvaNeural",
        "textHash": "c4279601863ae62b10993d26d861278eb98dc6eaa099b633a9840e82d92bf453",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 592,
        "sourceEnd": 879,
        "text": "No, not at all. Novelty is not bad. It is just not the same as effectiveness. Novelty means something feels new and different. Effectiveness means something actually works and produces results. A new method can be exciting, but if it does not solve the problem, it is only entertainment.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "d0b57af1d3c50e75fb9ff91d33310bc62e069fa87ca10e210cca434c4143f8a9",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 881,
        "sourceEnd": 1046,
        "text": "Student B (David): But sometimes a new method is the only way to get better results. For example, online classes were new for many teachers, and now they are normal.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AvaNeural",
        "textHash": "6fafc49ac28a2f38bcfba58184f6e17554900ef3014e8bc52647e56c8af98523",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 1063,
        "sourceEnd": 1322,
        "text": "That is a fair point, David. However, online classes became useful only after teachers changed how they taught. The technology was new, but the effectiveness came from better planning. So novelty can open a door, but it does not walk through the door for you.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "dfa094494f8860218e7930208b86d55526517cbbdfda3a76383e21423fc0885e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1324,
        "sourceEnd": 1486,
        "text": "Student C (Sophie): I think I disagree a little. If people are bored, they will not try anything. A new idea can make people interested. Isn't interest important?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AvaNeural",
        "textHash": "809f3d9c6630e86017293d7f4f82f8f98382176906985f3fde233efb5399953a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 1503,
        "sourceEnd": 1934,
        "text": "Yes, interest matters. But interest is a starting point, not a finish line. Let me give you another example. A hospital tried a new system for organizing medicine. It looked modern and everyone was excited. After three months, nurses made more mistakes, not fewer. The system was new, but it was not effective. The hospital then went back to an older system, with small changes. The older system was not exciting, but it was safer.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "30a8f649b3fd7b3c6a60e6be49b02abec8256d03c4367b1291b5d25754e0c6fc",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student a",
        "speaker": "Student A",
        "sourceStart": 1947,
        "sourceEnd": 1994,
        "text": "So the old system was better because it worked?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "78cc532a7801c9da8237a4162b877b3dbe8ac93bf1358da9d6a76758954a968d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2011,
        "sourceEnd": 2257,
        "text": "Exactly. The old system was more effective. It was less novel, but it was more useful. This is the key difference. We often confuse \"new\" with \"better.\" But new and better are two different questions. Ask first: Does it work? Then ask: Is it new?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "98bcafc8ad5d5f1bd80aaec5906c8ad1d82270c83044b8c373808edbdeba3d08",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student b",
        "speaker": "Student B",
        "sourceStart": 2270,
        "sourceEnd": 2323,
        "text": "What about when a new idea is both new and effective?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "0db5f2bd2b11b79412080000304291e7db35c22a1239cc01a111924802fb6685",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2340,
        "sourceEnd": 2588,
        "text": "That is the best case. But it is rare. Most new ideas need time and testing. A new idea is a hypothesis, not a result. You must test it. If it works, keep it. If it does not work, change it or drop it. Do not keep a bad idea just because it is new.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "0feee46c11a9302381911f519ec03b883414c673c0feb7826ef572a14e0d2b9a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student c",
        "speaker": "Student C",
        "sourceStart": 2601,
        "sourceEnd": 2668,
        "text": "But some people say we should always try new things. Is that wrong?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "8dc0ebd50296e151bb4cc43d06f24e326b953f2de9d5800fe6a16ef038d10cc0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2685,
        "sourceEnd": 2930,
        "text": "Trying new things is good, but trying is not the same as keeping. You can try a new idea and still decide it is not effective. That is not failure. That is learning. The danger is when we love novelty so much that we forget to ask about results.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "04ba387a0bd9b165e9dc022e985940671d8b9998670098a4e9e05241c8430278",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student a",
        "speaker": "Student A",
        "sourceStart": 2943,
        "sourceEnd": 2963,
        "text": "So how do we decide?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "87cf166866270bd6f40a997b3939666676189579ac49abc2550c3d3bb5629d38",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2980,
        "sourceEnd": 3266,
        "text": "Use a simple test. First, define the problem clearly. Second, try the new idea on a small scale. Third, measure the results. Fourth, compare with the old way. If the new way is better, use it. If not, do not use it just because it is new. This test is not exciting, but it is effective.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "588478badbcd83805f0dcc5ca856280f1b7fdb35c5fa6e5a344b481b856befa8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "student b",
        "speaker": "Student B",
        "sourceStart": 3279,
        "sourceEnd": 3296,
        "text": "That sounds slow.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "e4fa28de70585abeb3549bd7b380c1be7afee3f6e5348bfbdf6a95ff9c3885ac",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "professor lin",
        "speaker": "Professor Lin",
        "sourceStart": 3313,
        "sourceEnd": 3533,
        "text": "It can be slow. But slow and careful is often better than fast and wrong. Remember: novelty gets attention. Effectiveness gets results. We need both, but we need effectiveness more. Thank you. We will continue next week.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "5a641cfea27ff0f39c35a1e5e08ce50d7cba915c9227804f403b6c976839e5ad",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
